// THE DECLARED CARGO BUILD OPERATION: one named effect a native (emitted) program may be given, as
// the performing twin of extdeps.rust.cargo_build. That module BUILDS argv and manifest vocabulary for
// workflows, CI and release recipes; this one DECLARES the single operation and is what the native
// compiler-route denominator admits (gunbc.design_ruling native_compiler_route_admits_the_declared_cargo_build).
// It is its own module for the reason extdeps.docker.cli_service states at its head (2026-10-25): a
// program that PERFORMS must not be fused to the module that assembles the argv and JSON vocabulary for
// other hosts, because emission is per module -- one .rs per module, measured at the 2026-11-01 native
// claim emission (222 module files in the emitted crate) -- so importing one function here would carry
// that module's whole closure into every native build. The identity of the program therefore lives HERE
// and extdeps.rust.cargo_build cargo_program() delegates to it: the word "cargo" is minted once.
//
// THE BOUNDARY, STATED HONESTLY. This is the declared Cargo build operation, NOT "no subprocess
// execution": cargo runs the package's build scripts (build.rs) and proc-macro crates, and this module
// declares that rather than hiding it. What is bounded is the INVOCATION. The argv SHAPE is fixed here;
// every variable part is a named parameter; and the program position can only ever be the declared cargo
// identity -- never an executable because someone wrote a path or a string. The contrast is deliberate:
// extdeps.shell.exec is the generic-exec service (its program is a NonEmptyStr parameter), it stays
// outside the compiler-route denominator, and this service exists so that no route needs it.
//
// WHERE THE PROGRAM COMES FROM, BY CITED AUTHORITY. `cargo_build_program` below is the one identity this
// operation will run, and its mint is admitted in extdeps.exec.program's roster, which that module
// describes as "the census of every program gunbc invokes through a typed argv"; adding or moving an
// entry there is a counted review event, which is why this is a delegation and not a second mint. WHERE
// that word resolves is not this module's fact: gunbc.toolchain_workflow_steps gives each job its own
// HOME/CARGO_HOME/RUSTUP_HOME, gunbc.toolchain_home_standing makes that an obligation whose absence is
// the ToolchainHomesNotIsolated refusal, and src/v2/workflow/ci_workflow_run_emit
// ci_pin_rustup_cargo_bin_resolve_command takes $CARGO_HOME/bin/cargo when the isolated shim is present
// and refuses through ci_pin_rustup_cargo_bin_missing_command when neither it nor PATH carries one. Those
// are shims dispatching to the ACTIVE toolchain (extdeps.rust.rustup: "toolchain is a property, not the
// machine"), which is why the identity is a name and not a path.
//
// CWD AND ENV, DECLARED RATHER THAN INHERITED BY ACCIDENT. The operation declares NO environment of its
// own: the process sees exactly what the seed-build preamble put there and nothing this module adds --
// contrast the `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=` assignment extdeps.rust.cargo_build STILL pushes through `env` at its
// three build call sites (cargo.Build `BuildManifest`, `BuildManifestMessages`, `CheckManifestMessages`):
// this operation names its output directory in the argv, the workflow route does not yet, and that
// leftover is filed, not fixed here -- gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
// build_target_directory_declared_only_through_injected_env.
//
// THE WORKING ROOT IS NOT A CARGO FLAG, MEASURED, AND THAT IS WHY IT SITS IN THE BINDING. On the pinned
// toolchain (rust-toolchain.toml channel 1.93.0) `cargo build --workdir p` answers "unexpected argument
// '--workdir' found" and `cargo -C p build` answers "the `-C` flag is unstable, pass -Z unstable-options
// on the nightly channel" (measured 2026-11-02). An argv token for the root would therefore fail at
// cargo's own parser, so the route declares the root where it can be enforced: the native entry receives
// its working root as an explicit typed argument beside its source roots, and the emitted binding
// constructs the process config from it (operator ruling, option (c)); absence REFUSES at startup with a
// typed cause and never falls back to ".". That binding is the ruled-but-unbuilt half of this work: it
// sits in v1.compiler.emit_rust native_effect_binding_of, which today constructs every admitted service
// with `Type::new(Default::default())`, leaving extdeps.transports.shell ShellTransportConfig.working_dir
// None and the process in "." (that ambient default on the sibling route is filed as
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode admitted_shell_effect_spawned_at_an_ambient_working_root). Nothing in this
// module claims that binding exists.

module extdeps.rust.cargo_build_service

import std.types { Int, NonEmptyStr, String, Unit }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }
import extdeps.exec.program { ProgramIdentity, uncataloged_program }

data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo-build.html"
  }
}

// THE ONE CARGO IDENTITY the native route runs. extdeps.rust.cargo_build cargo_program() delegates here,
// and this is the row extdeps.exec.program's uncataloged roster names.
fn cargo_build_program() -> ProgramIdentity = uncataloged_program(invocation: "cargo")

// THE FIXED argv OF THE DECLARED OPERATION, part by part, so the shape is one row each and a widened
// invocation is an edit a reviewer can see. The flag names are rows, not strings at a call site.
data cargo_build_subcommand: NonEmptyStr = "build"

data cargo_build_manifest_path_flag: NonEmptyStr = "--manifest-path"

data cargo_build_target_dir_flag: NonEmptyStr = "--target-dir"

data cargo_build_profile_flag: NonEmptyStr = "--profile"

data cargo_build_package_flag: NonEmptyStr = "--package"

data cargo_build_bin_flag: NonEmptyStr = "--bin"

// THE PROFILE WORDS of `cargo build --profile`. A profile is a closed pair of names here, not a string at
// a call site: a new profile is a row in this module and a widening of the sum, which is the review event.
type CargoBuildProfile = ProfileRelease | ProfileDev

data cargo_build_profile_release_word: NonEmptyStr = "release"

data cargo_build_profile_dev_word: NonEmptyStr = "dev"

fn cargo_build_profile_word(profile: CargoBuildProfile) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match profile {
    ProfileRelease => cargo_build_profile_release_word
    ProfileDev => cargo_build_profile_dev_word
  }
}

// THE PROGRAM POSITION IS NOT OPEN, AND A GUARD IS NOT ENOUGH TO SAY SO.
//
// `CargoProgramWord` is sole_constructor: this module builds the only one there is, and a caller cannot
// spell one. The only route to it is cargo_build_program_word below, which yields a word for the declared
// cargo identity and a refusal arm for anything else -- so a caller holding a path, a raw string, or
// another program's identity has no expression that reaches CargoBuild's `program` input. The refusal
// travels as a returned arm, which is what lets a claim read it; the operation itself never sees a word
// that was not checked.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, STATED BECAUSE THE GAP IS REAL AND MEASURED: typing does not by itself refuse a
// string handed to a declared-type position. That exemption is recorded and rung-honest in
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode refined_alias_argument_exempt_from_declared_type_inhabitance, and the
// admission witness in dag/test/claim/cargo_build_native_route_witness.dag reports what the checker does
// with a raw string in that position rather than assuming the answer.
type CargoProgramWord sole_constructor { word: NonEmptyStr }

data cargo_program_word_not_cargo_cause: NonEmptyStr =
  "the program is not the declared cargo identity extdeps.rust.cargo_build_service cargo_build_program"

type CargoProgramWordResolution
  = CargoProgramWordResolved { word: CargoProgramWord }
  | CargoProgramWordRefused { program: ProgramIdentity, cause: NonEmptyStr }

// The comparison is identity-to-identity, never to a literal: the right-hand side is the declared row
// itself, so this function cannot drift from the program the roster mints.
fn cargo_build_program_word(program: ProgramIdentity) -> CargoProgramWordResolution {
  if program.invocation == cargo_build_program().invocation {
    CargoProgramWordResolved { word: CargoProgramWord { word: program.invocation } }
  } else {
    CargoProgramWordRefused { program: program, cause: cargo_program_word_not_cargo_cause }
  }
}

// The sealed word's only projection, and the only way argv ever sees it.
fn cargo_program_word_text(word: CargoProgramWord) -> NonEmptyStr = word.word

service CargoBuild {
  operation Build {
    requires opaque
    input {
      program: CargoProgramWord
      manifest_path: NonEmptyStr
      profile: CargoBuildProfile
      target_dir: NonEmptyStr
      package: NonEmptyStr
      bin: NonEmptyStr
    }

    // A build that ran and failed is an ANSWER read from these fields, not a sentinel exit code: the three
    // keys are modelled shell channels (v1.compiler.emit names the ten accepted keys at
    // src/v1/05_emit.dag:1445 -- stdout, stderr, exit_success, success, exists, exit_code, stdout_lines,
    // stderr_truncated, stderr_total_bytes, stderr_retained_bytes), and these are the ones the success and
    // nonzero-exit controls read.
    //
    // The typed extdeps.transports.shell ShellOutcome sum is NOT one of those keys, and this module learned
    // that by being refused, not by being told: a compiler-route entry reaching this operation refused at
    // emit with 'native effect realization refused for entry nefx.cli_probe.v2_cli_main': service
    // extdeps.rust.cargo_build_service CargoBuild has no native realization: operation 'Build' binds no
    // transport: shell transport output key 'outcome' has no modeled channel'. So a cargo that never
    // spawned stays a hard evaluation refusal here (the interpreter's own shell-spawn-refused diagnostic
    // points the reader at a ShellOutcome output field the native realization cannot lower), and the four
    // corpus modules that declare one -- extdeps.shell.exec, extdeps.github.ci_runner,
    // extdeps.github.organizations, extdeps.systemd.systemctl -- have never been realized natively. That
    // gap is filed as gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
    // transport_output_field_without_a_native_realization_channel; it is not papered over by declaring the
    // field and letting the emission refuse.
    output {
      exit_code: Int from "exit_code"
      stdout: String from "stdout"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }

    transport shell {
      argv: [
        cargo_program_word_text(word: program),
        cargo_build_subcommand,
        cargo_build_manifest_path_flag,
        manifest_path,
        cargo_build_target_dir_flag,
        target_dir,
        cargo_build_profile_flag,
        cargo_build_profile_word(profile: profile),
        cargo_build_package_flag,
        package,
        cargo_build_bin_flag,
        bin
      ]
    }

    exit {
      0 => Unit
      nonzero => Unit
    }
  }
}
